WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:12.980
Users and internal teams should understand whether prompt history is saved, who can access it, how long it is retained, whether it can be exported, and whether it may be reused for evaluation, training, or improvement.

2
00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:16.200
Transparency reduces unsafe assumptions.

3
00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:25.820
A user who believes a prompt disappears after the browser tab closes may share different information than a user who understands that records are retained for support or security review.

4
00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:38.740
Transparency can appear in product settings, privacy notices, admin documentation, employee guidance, acceptable use policies, support playbooks, and customer-facing controls.

5
00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:42.760
It should be specific enough to be useful without overwhelming the audience.

6
00:00:42.860 --> 00:00:48.140
For example, an administrator needs configuration detail and vendor commitments.

7
00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:54.400
An end user may need clear statements about history, deletion, sensitive data, and safe use.

8
00:00:54.680 --> 00:00:57.240
Governance also needs named owners.

9
00:00:57.340 --> 00:01:09.020
Someone should own retention settings, access reviews, deletion workflows, export controls, reuse approvals, incident response, vendor review, and periodic policy updates.

10
00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:20.080
Without ownership, prompt history governance becomes scattered across product, engineering, security, privacy, legal, compliance, support, and procurement.

11
00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:25.060
Shared responsibility is healthy, but each decision still needs an accountable path.

12
00:01:25.340 --> 00:01:27.500
Ownership must include ongoing review.

13
00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:35.840
AI platforms, vendor defaults, model gateways, plugins, observability pipelines, and analytics tools can change.

14
00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:44.020
A setting that was appropriate at launch may drift after a new feature, integration, region, model, or support workflow appears.

15
00:01:44.120 --> 00:01:53.560
Regular governance reviews help teams verify that documentation, configuration, contracts, access controls, and real system behavior still match.

16
00:01:53.660 --> 00:01:59.660
Transparency and ownership turn retention from a one-time setup task into a maintained practice.
